propsSchema.description:
Interactive map: a Mapbox basemap with every data layer (mvt, raster, geojson, h3, heatmap, arc, scatterplot, deck-geojson, fused-hex) rendered through deck.gl, plus `composite` level-of-detail layers made of several layers (any type), one per zoom range. Layers load data from a UDF directly (`udf`), from a tile endpoint (`tileUrl`) or from a Fused server's hex query endpoints (`fused-hex` with `source`). A DuckDB query (`sql`) reads UDF results on its own, or queries the rows of a `udf` or `fused-hex` layer (once over the tiles in view when the layer is tiled). A sidebar lets viewers restyle, filter and re-query layers at runtime.

## Example — UDF result (GeoDataFrame)

```json
{
  "type": "fused-map",
  "props": {
    "layers": [{
      "id": "buildings",
      "type": "geojson",
      "udf": "{{my_udf}}",
      "tooltip": true,
      "style": { "fillColor": { "type": "continuous", "attr": "height", "palette": "Sunset" } }
    }]
  }
}
```

## Example — UDF as H3 tiles

```json
{
  "type": "fused-map",
  "props": {
    "layers": [{
      "id": "hexes",
      "type": "h3",
      "udf": "{{my_h3_udf}}",
      "tile": true,
      "style": { "fillColor": { "type": "continuous", "attr": "count", "domain": [0, 500] } }
    }]
  }
}
```

## Example — MVT tile layer

```json
{
  "type": "fused-map",
  "props": {
    "layers": [{
      "id": "counties",
      "type": "mvt",
      "tileUrl": "https://tiles.fused.io/public/my_tileset/{z}/{x}/{y}",
      "style": { "fillColor": [100, 150, 200], "opacity": 0.6 }
    }]
  }
}
```

## Example — rows from a canvas SQL source (h3-query, sql-runner)

```json
{
  "type": "fused-map",
  "props": {
    "layers": [{
      "id": "calls",
      "type": "h3",
      "h3Column": "hex",
      "data": "{{nyc311}}",
      "sql": "SELECT hex, COUNT(*) AS calls FROM {{source}} GROUP BY 1",
      "style": { "fillColor": { "type": "continuous", "attr": "calls" } }
    }]
  }
}
```

`data: "{{name}}"` draws the rows a canvas SQL source published (an h3-query or sql-runner widget's `name`, or another map's layer id) straight from DuckDB: no UDF runs, and the edge from that node to this one grants access, as for a UDF. Add `sql` to query them (`{{source}}` or `{{name}}` reads the rows). Row layers only (geojson, h3, scatterplot, heatmap, arc, or type omitted to detect), never tiles.

## Example — SQL with lat/lng points and a filter

```json
{
  "type": "fused-map",
  "props": {
    "layers": [{
      "id": "sites",
      "type": "geojson",
      "sql": "SELECT name, lat, lng, score FROM {{my_udf}}",
      "filters": [{ "attr": "score", "type": "range", "min": 0.5 }],
      "style": { "fillColor": [255, 80, 0], "pointRadius": 8 }
    }]
  }
}
```

## Example — raster tile layer

```json
{
  "type": "fused-map",
  "props": {
    "layers": [{
      "id": "seismic",
      "type": "raster",
      "tileUrl": "https://udf.ai/<token>/run/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}?format=png",
      "style": { "opacity": 0.8 }
    }]
  }
}
```

## Example — indexed H3 data (fused-hex)

```json
{
  "type": "fused-map",
  "props": {
    "centerLng": -73.985, "centerLat": 40.755, "zoom": 14,
    "layers": [{
      "id": "nyc311-hex",
      "type": "fused-hex",
      "h3Column": "hex",
      "minZoom": 14,
      "source": {
        "path": "s3://fused-asset/data/nyc311/",
        "timeColumn": "created_date",
        "dateMin": "2024-07-01",
        "dateMax": "2024-07-07T23:59:59",
        "variables": ["complaint_type"]
      },
      "tooltip": ["hex", "created_date", "complaint_type"],
      "style": { "fillColor": { "type": "categorical", "attr": "complaint_type", "palette": "Bold" } }
    }]
  }
}
```

## Example — level of detail (composite)

One layer made of several layers (`levels`), each bound to a map zoom range: coarse aggregates zoomed out, fine cells or shapes zoomed in. Exactly one level draws at a time, the one whose `[minZoom, maxZoom)` holds the map zoom (on an overlap the highest minZoom wins; a zoom no level covers draws nothing). Crossing into another level keeps the previous one drawing until the new one has loaded the view (a tiled level: its tiles in view; a row level: its rows), then they swap in one frame, so the map never flashes empty; use this rather than several zoom-gated layers. **A level is a layer**: any type but composite (`fused-hex`, `fused-bbox`, `geojson`, `scatterplot`, `h3`, `heatmap`, `arc`, `mvt`, `raster`, `deck-geojson`), fed by that type's own keys (`source`, `udf` with `tile`, `data`, `sql`, `tileUrl`, ...), read and drawn exactly as that layer alone. A level without a `type` is `fused-hex` when it (or the composite) names a `source` path or endpoint, else detected from its data. The composite holds the defaults and a level stores only what differs, for every layer key: objects (`style`, `source`, `legend`) merge key by key over the composite's, a `fillColor` or `lineColor` replaces the composite's whole, arrays (`filters`, `tooltip`) and plain values (`sql`, `udf`, `type`, ...) replace, and `null` unsets the composite's value at that level (`"sql": null`: no query there). The composite's own `source` holds options every source level shares; `path` and `endpoint` are always the level's. `visible`, `visibleMinZoom` and `visibleMaxZoom` are the composite's only. Give each level an `id` (letters, digits, `_`, `-`; unique in the composite) so editing the list keeps the level on screen. A composite is two canvas SQL sources, `{{id}}` and `{{id_drawn}}`, following the level on screen: for a fused-hex level the raw rows of its tiles in view and the rows it draws; for a row level its rows and its filtered rows; empty for mvt and raster levels. A widget in the same node JSON reads them without an edge.

```json
{
  "id": "places", "name": "Places", "type": "composite", "h3Column": "hex",
  "tooltip": true,
  "style": { "fillColor": { "type": "continuous", "attr": "n", "palette": "Sunset", "scaleType": "quantile" } },
  "levels": [
    { "id": "z0", "maxZoom": 8, "source": { "path": "s3://bucket/places/hex5/" } },
    { "id": "z8", "minZoom": 8, "maxZoom": 13, "type": "scatterplot", "data": "{{place_points}}",
      "sql": "SELECT * FROM {{place_points}} WHERE n > 10", "style": { "pointRadius": 3 } },
    { "id": "z13", "minZoom": 13, "type": "geojson", "udf": "{{place_shapes}}", "tile": true }
  ]
}
```

Raster layers with a `tileUrl` fetch PNG tiles from a publicly reachable XYZ endpoint; only `opacity` and `visible` apply. `udf` with `tile: true` instead runs the UDF per tile through the workbench (or the canvas share token on a shared page), so no share token is needed and edits to the UDF re-render the layer.

## Choosing a source

- `udf` without `tile`: one whole run. Rows become features (geojson, h3, heatmap, arc, scatterplot); an image result becomes one `raster` picture at the bounds the run returned or was sent. Not valid for `mvt` — that needs `tile: true` with `udf`, or a `tileUrl`.
- `udf` with `tile: true`: the UDF runs per XYZ tile; use for large or tile-shaped UDFs (`raster` → PNG, `h3` → hexagons, `geojson` → vector features). Tile layers never auto-fit.
- `sql`: a DuckDB query over `{{udf}}` results; rows become features via geometryColumn / latColumn+lngColumn / h3Column. On a tiled layer (`udf` with `tile: true`, or `fused-hex`) it runs once over the rows of every tile loaded in the current view, reading them as `{{source}}`, which takes no `?params` (a udf layer may also write its own `{{udf}}`, bare or with exactly its own `?params`), and runs again as the view moves (pan, zoom), the query changes or a `$param` changes; no tile is refetched. WHERE, computed columns, GROUP BY, aggregates, LIMIT, ORDER BY and USING SAMPLE apply to the whole view; only a single SELECT (a WITH is fine) over `{{source}}` runs (no UNION, several statements, other UDFs or joins), and its result must keep the cell column (h3, `fused-hex`; a composite level drawn as `scatterplot` or `geojson` may return lat/lng or a geometry column instead) or the geometry columns (vector layers). `raster`, `mvt` and `tileUrl` layers take no `sql`.
- `source` (`fused-hex` only): a Fused server's ERA5, gridMET or `/h3/query` endpoint, queried per XYZ tile; no `udf` (a `sql` runs once over the tiles in view as `{{source}}`).
- Whole-run results larger than the viewer's "Max amount of data to auto-load" setting (default 50 MB) are not loaded until the viewer confirms, and UDFs marked for manual runs show a Run button instead of running on load. Prefer `tile: true`, a narrowing `sql`, or both for big datasets.

## Layer types

- `geojson`: polygons, lines and points from any source. `deck-geojson` is an alias of `geojson` and renders identically — prefer `geojson`.
- `h3`: hexagons from `h3Column` (auto-detected from common names like hex, h3 when omitted), styled with `coverage`, `extruded`, `elevationAttr`; `coverageAttr` sizes each cell by a numeric column (mapped from `coverageDomain` onto `coverageRange`, times `coverage`; fused-hex too).
- `scatterplot`: point circles; `pointRadius` in pixels (default 5), `lineColor`/`lineWidth` for the outline.
- `heatmap`: density of points; weight comes from a continuous `fillColor.attr` (1 per point when fillColor is static), `pointRadius` is the kernel radius in pixels (default 30). Not pickable, no tooltip.
- `arc`: one arc per row. Give the endpoints with `startLatColumn`/`startLngColumn` and `endLatColumn`/`endLngColumn` (auto-detected from common names when omitted), or a `source`/`target` property holding [lng, lat], or a LineString whose first and second vertices are the ends. `fillColor` colours the source end, `lineColor` the target end, `lineWidth` the arc width.
- `mvt` / `raster`: tiled from `tileUrl`, or from `udf` with `tile: true`.
- `fused-hex`: H3 tiles queried from a Fused server; each XYZ tile's bbox is sent to `source.endpoint` (a full URL, or a path on the current server; /h3/query when only `source.path` is set): `/retrieve/era5/daily/bbox`, `/retrieve/era5/hourly/bbox` and `/retrieve/gridmet/bbox` serve fixed resolution 7 datasets and need `dateMin`/`dateMax`; `/h3/query` reads any indexed `source.path` and needs the layer's `h3Column` set to the location's indexed space column (set `source.timeColumn` too so latest/first can order rows). Each hex's rows over the window are reduced to one (`reduce`: latest, first, mean, min, max, sum). Tiles load from zoom 7 for a single day; unless `minZoom` is set the floor rises with the window (about one zoom level per 4x more time steps) so a tile stays within the server's row limit, and nothing draws below it. A tile the server still rejects as too large shows an error: zoom in or narrow the window. Row cells cover their hexagon whole unless `style.coverage` is set (default 1, where other hex layers default to 0.9). It reads `source` instead of `udf`; `sql` runs once over every tile in view as `{{source}}`, before `reduce`. It is never auto-detected, never auto-fits and takes no `bounds`. Set `source.overview` (`{}` is enough) to outline, from zoom 3 up to the floor, the H3 cells of the files the query reads (no rows are read): one request lists them, with no tiles and no values, so the outlines show where data exists, not what it holds. ERA5 retrieve endpoints and /h3/query only (for /h3/query the world extent lists the whole location); it is an error on gridMET. `overview.minZoom` (default 3) must sit below the floor for the overview to draw, and `overview.extent` limits the area it looks up and also confines the row tiles: none are requested outside it, and cells are drawn only inside it, overview and rows alike, by cell centre (default: the whole world). Overview cells are styled apart from the rows by `style.overview` (fused-hex only): `lineColor` and `fillColor` (default: #afafaf stroke, no fill) each take a single colour, `"inherit"` (the layer's own stroke/fill; when that is data-driven, no overview is drawn and a warning shows on the layer card, rows unaffected), or a data-driven `{type: "continuous"|"categorical", attr, ...}` colouring cells by `row_count_total` (rows in the whole file) or `row_count_window` (rows in the file sections the current date window reads) -- the only two overview attributes, since a files overview carries no other values. Also `lineWidth` (default 1, 0 = no stroke), `opacity` (the fill's, default 0.35) and `coverage` (default 1).

## Colours

`style.fillColor` and `style.lineColor` accept an [r, g, b] or [r, g, b, a] array, a CSS colour string, or a data-driven object:

- Continuous: `{ "type": "continuous", "attr": "<numeric property>", "domain": [min, max], "palette": "Sunset", "steps": 7, "reverse": false, "nullColor": "#b8b8b8", "scaleType": "quantize" }`. `domain` defaults to the data's min/max, `palette` to "Sunset". `scaleType` "quantize" (default) draws `steps` equal-width bands over the domain; "quantile" draws `steps` bands of roughly equal row count, with breaks from the data (`domain` is ignored). Continuous palettes: Sunset, SunsetDark, Teal, TealGrn, Emrld, BluGrn, BluYl, Mint, DarkMint, Purp, PurpOr, Magenta, Burg, BurgYl, RedOr, OrYel, Peach, PinkYl, BrwnYl, Uber Viz Sequential, Global Warming, Sunrise, Ocean Green, Pink Wine, Purple Blue Yellow, Violet Ocean, Summer Sky, Uber Viz Diverging, UberPool, Ice And Fire, BuGn, BuPu, GnBu, OrRd, PuBu, PuBuGn, PuRd, RdPu, YlGn, YlGnBu, YlOrBr, YlOrRd, Blues, Greens, Greys, Oranges, Purples, Reds, BrBG, PiYG, PRGn, PuOr, RdBu, RdGy, RdYlBu, RdYlGn, Spectral, Turbo, Viridis, Inferno, Magma, Plasma, Cividis, Warm, Cool, CubehelixDefault, Sinebow, Rainbow. Of these, Uber Viz Diverging, UberPool, Ice And Fire, BrBG, PiYG, PRGn, PuOr, RdBu, RdGy, RdYlBu, RdYlGn, Spectral are diverging.
- Categorical: `{ "type": "categorical", "attr": "<property>", "colors": { "<value>": "#hex" }, "palette": "Bold", "nullColor": "rgba(128,128,128,0.5)" }`. Omit `colors` to auto-assign from the palette (default "Bold"). Categorical palettes: Bold, Vivid, Safe, Prism, Uber Viz Qualitative, Tol Bright, Tol Vibrant, Tol Muted, Tol Medium Contrast, Tol Light, Okabe Ito, Accent, Dark2, Paired, Pastel1, Pastel2, Set1, Set2, Set3, Tableau10. Any other palette name is invalid.

A legend is drawn only for data-driven colours (see `legend` and `showLegend`).

`layerBlending` (`normal`, `additive`, `subtractive`) sets how all data layers blend where they overlap, as kepler.gl's Layer blending does; omit it for normal.

## Filters

A layer's `filters` keep the features that pass every one: `{ "attr": "score", "type": "range", "min": 0.5 }`, `{ "attr": "kind", "type": "categories", "values": ["a", "b"] }`, or a time window `{ "attr": "datetime", "type": "time", "min": "2025-01-06", "max": "2025-01-13T12:00:00Z" }`. A time filter reads ISO dates and datetimes (no zone means UTC), common date text and epoch numbers; its bounds are ISO text, epoch milliseconds, or a `$param`. The sidebar types time columns as times and a time filter has a Time playback button: it opens a bar along the bottom of the map that plays the window across the data, as kepler.gl's time filter does (any layer with rows, tiled layers over their loaded tiles; not rasters). The bar's settings are kept on the filter as `playbackOptions` (mode auto | window | point | cumulative, skipEmpty, interval such as "1-day", timezone, timeFormat, yAxis { field, aggregation, fit }, collapsed, speed such as 2), every key optional. A fused-hex layer plays any time column. On its time column (source.timeColumn, date on the retrieve endpoints), when that is a series of daily or hourly steps, it plays one time step at a time: the window is one step, play moves 4 steps a second at 1× and skips steps with no rows, and `source.reduce` is ignored while the filter holds one step. A paused or closed bar leaves that step in the filter (min and max equal), and the layer keeps drawing it; Reset clears it and the layer reduces again.
